+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql
+
+`users.email` cannot be made `NOT NULL` and case-insensitively unique without
+modifying or deleting customer rows, which this task forbids (Support owns that
+data). The production snapshot already violates both requirements.
+
+## Evidence from `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`
+
+| Problem | Rows |
+| --- | --- |
+| Email is NULL | id 17 (`'Goran Okafor'`) |
+| Case-insensitive duplicate | ids 1, 4, 23 all have `ana.silva@example.com` (id 4 stores `Ana.Silva@example.com`) |
+
+Reproduce on the snapshot:
+
+```sql
+SELECT id FROM users WHERE email IS NULL;                      -- 17
+SELECT lower(email), group_concat(id) FROM users
+GROUP BY lower(email) HAVING count(*) > 1;                     -- ana.silva@example.com: 1,4,23
+```
+
+Consequences:
+
+- `ALTER TABLE`/table rebuild with `email TEXT NOT NULL` fails while id 17 has a NULL.
+- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users(lower(email))` fails with
+  `UNIQUE constraint failed: index ...` because of ids 1/4/23.
+- SQLite has no `IF`-style conditional DDL, so a single migration file cannot
+  both create these constraints on a fresh DB and skip/fix them on prod data
+  without editing rows.
+
+I did not add a trigger-based approximation (enforcing only on new writes) or a
+partial index: that would silently leave the existing rows violating the
+invariant while appearing to satisfy the requirement, and the task explicitly
+says not to work around the problem.
+
+## What this unblocks it
+
+Support must first remediate the data (pick the real owner for the shared
+address, and supply or merge the NULL-email account). A suggested, non-destructive
+reconciliation — no rows deleted, all three duplicate accounts retained:
+
+```sql
+-- Example only; Support decides the final addresses.
+UPDATE users SET email = 'ana.silva+4@example.com' WHERE id = 4;
+UPDATE users SET email = 'ana.silva+23@example.com' WHERE id = 23;
+UPDATE users SET email = 'goran.okafor@example.com' WHERE id = 17;
+```
+
+Once no NULLs or case-insensitive duplicates remain, `002_unique_email.sql` can
+be implemented as a table rebuild:
+
+```sql
+CREATE TABLE users_new (
+    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
+    email TEXT NOT NULL,
+    name TEXT
+);
+INSERT INTO users_new (id, email, name) SELECT id, email, name FROM users;
+DROP TABLE users;
+ALTER TABLE users_new RENAME TO users;
+CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_nocase ON users (lower(email));
+```
+
+Until then, `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` is intentionally left unimplemented
+and `test_migrations.py::test_constraint_on_fresh_db` fails (the prod-snapshot
+row-preservation test trivially passes because the migration changes nothing).
